  pages        = {1077--1081},
  date         = {1986-05-10},
  abstract     = {Although the rise in ischaemic heart disease in England and
                  Wales has been associated with increasing prosperity,
                  mortality rates are highest in the least affluent areas. On
                  division of the country into two hundred and twelve local
                  authority areas a strong geographical relation was found
                  between ischaemic heart disease mortality rates in 1968-78 and
                  infant mortality in 1921-25. Of the twenty-four other common
                  causes of death only bronchitis, stomach cancer, and rheumatic
                  heart disease were similarly related to infant mortality.
